<section><div class="wrap feature-lead"><div class="feature-copy"><span class="section-tag">Battery runway</span><h2>A window, or an honest silence.</h2><p>Once there is enough history, WhatBattery fits a trend through the Mac's recorded health and projects the window in which it may reach 80%, the point Apple uses as a service reference.</p><p>The harder part is knowing when not to answer. A battery's health reading is the fuel gauge's own estimate: it sits still for weeks, then steps when the gauge recalibrates. A few weeks of that can look like a steep decline and is not one. WhatBattery tests whether the trend is real before it will name anything, and says nothing when it is not.</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>No projection until the evidence supports one</li><li>A date range rather than false precision</li><li>Recent wear rate in plain language</li><li>Faster-than-trend warning</li></ul></div><img src="/img/screenshot-history.png" alt="Long-term battery health history in WhatBattery" width="440" /></div></section>

<section class="soft-section"><div class="wrap feature-lead"><img src="/img/screenshot-cells.png" alt="Per-cell voltage, capacity and resistance, with the pack's lifetime extremes, in WhatBattery Pro" width="440" loading="lazy" /><div class="feature-copy"><span class="section-tag">Cells and internals</span><h2>What the gauge knows and never shows you.</h2><p>Your battery's fuel gauge keeps per-cell readings, and macOS surfaces none of them. WhatBattery reads them straight out: each cell's voltage and learned capacity, how far apart they are drifting, and the extremes the pack has seen across its whole life, including before you installed anything.</p><p>Cell balance is the useful part. A pack that is genuinely failing usually shows one cell pulling away from its neighbours well before the headline health figure moves.</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>Per-cell voltage, capacity and resistance</li><li>Cell balance, called out in millivolts</li><li>When the gauge last recalibrated, which explains sudden health steps</li><li>Lifetime temperature, voltage and current extremes</li><li>Today's charge range, min to max, as the gauge itself logged it</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section class="soft-section"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="section-tag">Two kinds of history</span><h2>Inspect the session. Keep the lifetime record.</h2><p>Dense telemetry helps explain what happened during a charge or drain. Sparse monthly health records show the slower wear story across every device.</p></div><div class="card-grid two"><article class="card"><h3>Lifetime Analyzer</h3><p>Explore charge, temperature, voltage, and power over a selected range, with minimum, average, and maximum values. On a fresh install, the charge chart also imports days or weeks of history from macOS's own sleep and wake log, drawn as a distinct dashed line from anything WhatBattery has measured itself.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Health History</h3><p>Keep one durable record per device per day, rolled into monthly health and cycle summaries for Macs, iPhones, and iPads.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section id="reports"><div class="wrap feature-lead"><img src="/img/screenshot-report.png" alt="A one-page WhatBattery battery report ready to save as PDF or print" width="620" loading="lazy" /><div class="feature-copy"><span class="section-tag">Reports and export</span><h2>Keep the evidence when the battery matters.</h2><p>Turn the current reading and lifetime summary into a one-page PDF or printout for a warranty claim, a Genius Bar visit, or a resale listing.</p><p>For deeper analysis, export recorded samples as CSV or JSON. Health history can also be backed up and restored.</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>One-page PDF and print report</li><li>CSV and JSON sample export</li><li>Long-term health backup and restore</li></ul></div></div></section>

